{"id":"neet-2026-q007","pn":7,"y":"NEET 2026 · 3 May","tp":"Alternating Current","st":"Series LCR resonance","q":"An ac circuit contains a resistance of 1 kΩ, a capacitor of 0.1 μF and an inductor of 1 mH connected in series. The resonance frequency of the circuit is approximately.","o":["10.1 kHz","20.7 kHz","15.9 kHz","13.5 kHz"],"c":2,"s":"Resonance frequency f₀ = 1/(2π√(LC)).\nf₀ = 1/(2π√(1×10⁻⁷ × 10⁻³)).\nf₀ = 15.9 kHz."},
{"id":"neet-2026r-q012","pn":12,"y":"Re-NEET 2026 · 21 Jun","tp":"Alternating Current","st":"Series LCR resonance","q":"An ac voltage V = 220 sin(2 × 10³t) Volt is applied to a series LCR circuit. Then the current amplitude in this circuit is:\n(Given : L = 10 mH, C = 25 μF, R = 100 Ω)","o":["5.5 A","11.0 A","22.0 A","2.2 A"],"c":3,"s":"Angular frequency of the source ω = 2 × 10³ rad/s.\nInductive reactance X_L = ωL = (2 × 10³) × (10 × 10⁻³) = 20 Ω.\nCapacitive reactance X_C = 1/(ωC) = 1/[(2 × 10³) × (25 × 10⁻⁶)] = 20 Ω.\nX_L = X_C, so the circuit is at resonance and impedance Z = R.\nCurrent amplitude i₀ = V₀/Z = V₀/R = 220/100 = 2.2 A."},
{"id":"neet-2026-q033","pn":33,"y":"NEET 2026 · 3 May","tp":"Alternating Current","st":"AC instantaneous value","q":"The peak value of an alternating current is 5 A and frequency is 60 Hz. How long will the current, starting from zero, take to reach the peak value?","o":["1/240 s","1/30 s","1/120 s","1/60 s"],"c":0,"s":"Alternating current: i = i_peak sin(ωt).\nHere ω = 2πf = 2π × 60 = 120π rad/s.\nSo i = 5 sin(120πt).\nFor the peak value 5 = 5 sin(120πt), so sin(120πt) = 1 = sin(π/2).\nHence 120πt = π/2 and t = 1/240 s."},
{"id":"neet-2025-q016","pn":16,"y":"NEET 2025","tp":"Alternating Current","st":"Series LCR circuit","q":"To an ac power supply of 220 V at 50 Hz, a resistor of 20 Ω, a capacitor of reactance 25 Ω and an inductor of reactance 45 Ω are connected in series. The corresponding current in the circuit and the phase angle between the current and the voltage is, respectively","o":["7.8 A and 30°","7.8 A and 45°","15.6 A and 30°","15.6 A and 45°"],"c":1,"s":"X_L = 45 Ω, X_C = 25 Ω, R = 20 Ω\nI = 220/√((X_L − X_C)² + R²) = 220/√((45 − 25)² + 20²)\nI = 220/(2√2) = 11/√2 = 7.779 A\ntan φ = (X_L − X_C)/R = (45 − 25)/20 = 1\nφ = 45°"},
{"id":"neet-2024-q005","pn":5,"y":"NEET 2024 · 5 May","tp":"Alternating Current","st":"Transformer turns ratio","q":"In an ideal transformer, the turns ratio is Nₚ/Nₛ = 1/2. The ratio Vₛ : Vₚ is equal to (the symbols carry their usual meaning) -","o":["1 : 1","1 : 4","1 : 2","2 : 1"],"c":3,"s":"For a transformer, Vₛ/Vₚ = Nₛ/Nₚ.\nNₚ/Nₛ = 1/2, so Nₛ/Nₚ = 2.\nSo Vₛ : Vₚ = 2 : 1."},
{"id":"neet-2024-q045","pn":45,"y":"NEET 2024 · 5 May","tp":"Alternating Current","st":"Capacitor in AC circuit","q":"A 10 μF capacitor is connected to a 210 V, 50 Hz source as shown in figure. The peak current in the circuit is nearly (π = 3.14):","o":["1.20 A","0.35 A","0.58 A","0.93 A"],"c":3,"s":"Capacitive reactance X_C = 1/(ωC) = 1/(2πfC) = 1/(2 × 3.14 × 50 × 10 × 10⁻⁶) = 1000/3.14 Ω.\nV_rms = 210 V, so i_rms = V_rms/X_C = 210/X_C.\nPeak current = √2 × i_rms = √2 × (210/1000) × 3.14 = 0.932 ≈ 0.93 A.","fig":{"w":240,"h":205,"alt":"A capacitor C = 10 μF in the top wire of a rectangular circuit, left plate marked plus and right plate minus, and an AC source 210 V, 50 Hz in the bottom wire.","d":[["l",15,28,128,28],["l",143,28,235,28],["l",128,12,128,46],["l",143,12,143,46],["t",124,24,"+","e","s"],["t",124,41,"+","e","s"],["t",148,24,"−","s"],["t",148,41,"−","s"],["t",144,66,"C = 10 μF","m"],["l",15,28,15,167],["l",235,28,235,167],["l",15,167,87,167],["c",95,167,8],["t",95,170,"~","ms"],["l",103,167,235,167],["t",88,193,"210 V, 50 Hz"]]}}
